# "Weather" (rain, sun) for "whether" (if). Common-error lists; examples are ours.
# why: Whether means "if" (I'm not sure whether it's ready). Weather is rain, wind and sunshine.
expr asking [unsure, uncertain, wondering, wonder, wondered, wonders, decide, decides, decided, deciding, determine, determines, determined, doubt, doubtful, ask, asked, asking, asks, regardless, confirm, confirmed, sure, debate, debated, question, questioned, about, of, to]
expr main [<(@asking weather [the, it, they, we, you, he, she, I, this, that, there, our, your, their, or, to]), weather>, <(weather or not), weather>]

let message "Did you mean `whether` (if)?"
let description "Fixes `unsure weather it will rain` → `unsure whether it will rain`, and `weather or not`."
let kind "Malapropism"
let becomes "whether"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Officials are still unsure weather the law will pass." "Officials are still unsure whether the law will pass."
test "I wonder weather it will be ready." "I wonder whether it will be ready."
test "We'll go weather or not it rains." "We'll go whether or not it rains."

allows "We checked the weather before leaving."
allows "I'm unsure whether the law will pass."
allows "We talked about weather patterns."
allows "The weather or the traffic made us late."
